      "question": "A math teacher has a 5-inch piece of chalk that he uses to write equations on the chalkboard for his students. He is very conscious about conserving chalk and aims to use only 20% of the chalk each day. However, he cannot write with a piece of chalk that is smaller than 2 inches, so he recycles it when it reaches this size. On Monday, the teacher started with a new piece of chalk. His students needed extra help that day, so he ended up using 45% of the chalk by the end of the day. On Tuesday, he decided to use only 15% of the remaining chalk to compensate for the extra usage on Monday. From Wednesday onwards, he plans to go back to his usual practice of using 20% of the chalk each day. If he sticks to this plan, how many days will it be before he has to recycle this piece of chalk?",
      "source_context": null,
      "source_id": "gsm-plus-09411",
      "validation_failure": "unresolved reference 'chalk.days_from_monday_until_recycle' from scope 'global'",
      "validator_context": {
        "correct_answer": "5",
        "gold_reasoning": "On Monday, the teacher used 45% of the 5-inch chalk, which is 0.45 * 5 = 2.25 inches.\n\nSo, the remaining chalk after Monday is 5 - 2.25 = 2.75 inches.\n\nOn Tuesday, he used 15% of the remaining chalk, which is 0.15 * 2.75 = 0.4125 inches.\n\nSo, the remaining chalk after Tuesday is 2.75 - 0.4125 = 2.3375 inches.\n\nFrom Wednesday onwards, he used 20% of the chalk each day. \n\nLet's denote the number of days from Wednesday onwards as x. \n\nEach day, the chalk decreases by 20%, so the remaining chalk after x days is 2.3375 * (0.8)^x.\n\nThe teacher can use the chalk until it is 2 inches. So, we have the equation 2.3375 * (0.8)^x = 2.\n\nSolving this equation for x, we get x = log(2/2.3375) / log(0.8) \u2248 2.2.\n\nSince the teacher can't use a fraction of a day, we round 2.2 up to 3.\n\nSo, the teacher will have to recycle the chalk after Monday, Tuesday, and 3 more days, which is 2 (Monday and Tuesday) + 3 =  5 days.\n#### 5"
      }
